Yes. The mind’s eye is a misleading interface metaphor. It implies an internal camera facing forward. The mind’s I is the observer-model itself—and it need not possess a retina, forward direction, or single sensory channel.

Your brain already represents things outside its visual field: the chair behind you, the floor beneath you, your limbs without looking, the room beyond the wall. But it usually represents them as a sparse, multimodal scene graph, not as a fully rendered panoramic image. Attention renders whichever portion you query.

So the upgrade is not necessarily:

Produce a perfectly detailed 360° picture.

It is:

Maintain an addressable 360° world-model whose regions can be rendered through vision, proprioception, sound, touch, symbolism, or probability as needed.

Formally:

\text{Mind’s I}_t =
(\text{self-pose},\ \text{world-map},\ \text{sensorium},\
\text{memory},\ \text{uncertainty field},\ \text{attention cursor})

For every location x around the body:

W_t(x)=
\{\text{estimated contents},\text{relations},\text{possible sensations},
\text{confidence},\text{source}\}

Behind you need not initially “look” visual. It might exist as:

* a proprioceptively anchored direction,
* an object symbol attached to a location,
* a remembered spatial relationship,
* an expected sound or contact surface,
* and a probability distribution over what remains unknown.

Then attention can request a temporary rendering:

R = \operatorname{Render}(W_t,\ x,\ modality,\ resolution)

That is Resolution Modulation applied to embodied space. The entire sphere remains structurally present, while only the queried region receives expensive detail.

The crucial distinction

\text{representation} \neq \text{perception} \neq \text{reality}

A vivid reconstruction is not automatically more accurate than a vague one. So every projected element should carry provenance:

* sensed
* remembered
* inferred
* imagined
* unknown

This is the Salience Firewall inside the Mind’s I: vividness controls accessibility, while evidence controls confidence.

360° training operator

Close your eyes and establish six anchors:

\{\text{front, back, left, right, above, below}\}

Then:

1. Place remembered objects into those directions without forcing pictures.
2. Represent each as a fuzzy volume with a confidence level.
3. Add nonvisual properties: distance, expected sound, navigability, texture, danger.
4. Move the attention cursor behind you without mentally turning your physical eyes.
5. Rotate the modeled self while attempting to keep the room stationary.
6. Open your eyes or turn around and collect the delta.
7. Update the model rather than rewarding mere vividness.

Over time, additional anchors create a spherical spatial lattice. The objective is not hallucination-grade imagery; it is increasingly accurate allocentric presence—knowing the surrounding space independently of where the eyes point.

This also reveals something larger:

Each sense is only a viewing mode for the I.

Vision renders light structure. Hearing renders causal direction and distance. Proprioception renders the body’s geometry. Interoception renders its internal condition. Language renders symbolic relations. Imagination renders counterfactual possibilities. None owns the model.

Canonical handle: Mind’s I / Omnidirectional Self-Model

\boxed{\text{I am not the camera. I am the system that can position cameras—or operate without one.}}